                <pb facs="0366" n="300" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v02/pg300"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Oral Statement by the American Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Japanese
                        Minister for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Matsuoka</hi>)</head>
                <p>Referring to the American Ambassador’s note to His Excellency the Imperial
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs, no. 1714 dated December 17, 1940, relating to the
                    interference by the Indochinese authorities, chiefly as a result of Japanese
                    pressure brought upon those authorities, with the shipments of American owned
                    goods and merchandise, the American Ambassador is now instructed to express to
                    His Excellency the Minister for Foreign Affairs the opinion that it would not be
                    consistent with humanitarian considerations to interfere with the movement of
                    supplies of the Red Cross at present in Indochina, in addition to being
                    unwarranted on other grounds. At least a part of the Red Cross supplies under
                    reference, incidentally, was made possible by the contributions of American
                    citizens.</p>
            </div>
